          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What most reliably distinguishes a leprosy skin patch from conditions such as ti</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What most reliably distinguishes a leprosy skin patch from conditions such as tinea versicolor or vitiligo?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The colour of the patch</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypopigmentation occurs in many conditions including vitiligo, tinea versicolor, and post-inflammatory changes. Colour alone cannot distinguish leprosy from its mimics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of sensation within the patch</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Loss of sensation (anaesthesia) within a skin patch is the hallmark feature that separates leprosy from its common mimics, making simple sensory testing invaluable even at a rural clinic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The size and number of patches</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Patch size and number vary widely across the leprosy spectrum and also occur in many other dermatoses. They cannot reliably differentiate leprosy from mimics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of scaling or flaking</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Scaling is more typical of fungal infections like tinea. Leprosy patches are characteristically dry and smooth, but the defining feature is sensory loss, not surface texture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is loss of sensation within the patch. Many conditions produce hypopigmented patches, but only leprosy consistently causes anaesthesia within the lesion — making sensory testing essential when evaluating any suspicious skin change."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Sensory loss within a skin lesion is the single most reliable clinical feature separating leprosy from lookalike conditions. This is why sensory testing with a cotton wisp is the first diagnostic step for any suspicious pale patch."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
