          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A leprosy patient on MDT has no new skin changes and reports no complaints at fo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A leprosy patient on MDT has no new skin changes and reports no complaints at follow-up. However, routine examination reveals new weakness of thumb opposition. What is this phenomenon called?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Type 1 (reversal) reaction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A Type 1 reaction presents with inflamed, swollen existing skin lesions and often painful neuritis. This patient has no skin changes or pain — the nerve damage was found only on active testing.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Drug-induced neuropathy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MDT drugs do not typically cause motor neuropathy. This painless, isolated motor loss discovered on routine examination is characteristic of silent neuropathy in leprosy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silent neuropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Silent neuropathy is nerve function impairment that occurs without any pain, tenderness, or visible skin changes — it is detected only through routine motor and sensory testing at each visit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Relapse of leprosy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Relapse refers to reactivation of infection after completing treatment, typically presenting with new skin lesions or a rising bacterial index — not isolated painless motor loss during ongoing therapy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This is silent neuropathy — nerve function loss that occurs without pain, tenderness, or visible changes. It is the reason every leprosy follow-up must include formal nerve function assessment. Borderline patients (BT, BB, BL) are at highest risk."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Silent neuropathy is one of the most dangerous complications in leprosy because it causes permanent disability if missed. This is why every follow-up must include active sensory and motor testing — not just a symptom enquiry."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
