          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old woman with multibacillary leprosy on MDT develops severe, recurren</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman with multibacillary leprosy on MDT develops severe, recurrent erythema nodosum leprosum unresponsive to prednisolone. Which is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stop MDT and switch to a second-line regimen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MDT must NEVER be stopped during a lepra reaction. Reactions are immune-mediated events, not a sign of treatment failure — stopping MDT risks disease progression.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thalidomide 100 mg daily</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thalidomide is the most effective drug for severe ENL but is an absolute teratogen — it is CONTRAINDICATED in women of childbearing age and reserved for men and post-menopausal women only.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-dose clofazimine (300 mg/day)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. For severe or recurrent ENL in a woman of childbearing age, high-dose clofazimine (300 mg/day for 1–3 months, then tapered) is the appropriate choice, since thalidomide is absolutely contraindicated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Restart prednisolone at double the previous dose</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Simply escalating prednisolone is not recommended for steroid-refractory ENL. High-dose clofazimine (300 mg/day — six times the MDT dose of 50 mg) is the established next step.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is high-dose clofazimine (300 mg/day). Remember the two unbreakable rules: MDT always continues through a reaction, and thalidomide must never be given to a woman of childbearing age due to its teratogenic effects."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Two rules never bend in lepra reaction management: MDT continues throughout, and thalidomide is never given to women who could become pregnant. High-dose clofazimine (300 mg/day, tapered over months) is the safe alternative for severe ENL."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
